    "q-213230": {
      "dom_id": "step-q213230",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Which of the following relations are reflexive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x=y+2,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=0,$ defined on $Q$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $y={x}^{3},$ defined on $R$",
      "worked_solution": "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is irreflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx\\ne x+2\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in R.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is reflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\in S$ for all $x\\in Q.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}=0\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Q.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,2)\\notin T$ since $2\\ne {2}^{3},$ while\n\n- $(1,1)\\in T$ since $1={1}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\"",
